Added `invoke` tasks for the containerlab lab - `lab-up`, `lab-down`, `lab-populate`, `lab-consumer`, `lab-break`, `lab-events` and `lab-console` - and an `eventconsumer` task for running the consumer against any configured broker. The lab's compose overlay is added automatically when `EVENT_TRACKER_LAB` is set, so no `invoke.yml` editing is needed.
The containerlab population script now assigns fabric addresses read from each node's own startup configuration and cables every link between two devices, alongside the management addresses it already assigned.
The lab now runs `nautobot-server eventconsumer` as a compose service, so `invoke lab-up` brings up a Nautobot that is already consuming events. `lab: true` in `invoke.yml`, documented in `invoke.example.yml`, makes the lab and its consumer part of the environment plain `invoke start` brings up.
`generate_nautobot_event_tracker_test_data` now creates the containerlab lab's fabric device for device - the same names, interfaces, addresses and links - rather than an estate of its own that shared three device names with it and agreed about nothing else. A test holds the two together against the topology file and the nodes' own startup configurations.
The development stack now consumes events from the Redis it already runs, so `invoke start`, `invoke eventconsumer` and the new `invoke send-test-event` produce a ticket on any machine without containerlab. It reads the lab's topic configuration, so the field map a developer learns there is the one the lab uses.
containerlab is now a compose service of its own, `development/docker-compose.containerlab.yml`, added by `lab: true` alongside the broker's - so it can be named in `invoke.yml` like every other part of the environment, and the flags it needs are configuration rather than arguments buried in a task.
